<h2>The prosecution case</h2>
<p>A Narcotics Cell team, acting on a tip, apprehended the applicant on the morning of 9 April 2026 near Budhpur Service Road and recovered about 160 grams of smack from his bag; an FSL team confirmed the substance on the spot. Co-accused were arrested later with their own recoveries, which were not clubbed with the applicant&rsquo;s. The chargesheet was filed on 24 May 2026 in FIR No. 156/2026 (PS Alipur). By early October the applicant had spent close to six months in custody; the nominal roll recorded satisfactory conduct, and counsel pointed to his age &mdash; 20 years &mdash; and clean antecedents.</p>

<h2>Three layers of reasoning</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Quantity.</strong> 160 grams is intermediate quantity, so the Section 37 NDPS rigours did not apply &mdash; though the court was careful to say their absence is not itself a ticket to bail.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Custody and trial horizon.</strong> With the investigation complete and trial some distance away, indefinite incarceration of a young first-time accused sat uneasily with Article 21.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Grounds of arrest.</strong> The decisive layer: the arrest memo&rsquo;s solitary recital of the recovery was held not to amount to communication of grounds of arrest in the constitutional sense.</div>
</div>

<h2>What &ldquo;grounds of arrest&rdquo; now require</h2>
<p>The court&rsquo;s analysis tracks the Supreme Court&rsquo;s recent arc: grounds of arrest must be meaningful, must be communicated in writing, and must reach the arrestee in time to be of use &mdash; the two-hour-before-remand benchmark drawn from the recent case law was expressly invoked. A bare statement of what was recovered tells the arrestee nothing about the basis for the accusation against him specifically, and cannot enable him to oppose remand or arrange representation. The contrast within this very case was telling: the co-accused received written grounds; the applicant, on the record before the court, did not.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For the defence.</strong> Call for the arrest memo and remand papers in every bail matter. A recital-only memo, or an unproved claim of oral communication, is now a freestanding ground for bail even in NDPS cases.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For investigators.</strong> Grounds must be case-specific, in writing, acknowledged, and furnished before remand &mdash; and compliance must be provable from the record, not asserted from the bar.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The limits.</strong> The relief is release, not acquittal; the FSL report, recovery and conspiracy allegations remain live issues for trial.</div>
</div>
<p>The order adds to a consistent 2025&ndash;26 line in the Delhi High Court treating Article 22(1) compliance as a threshold question in bail hearings across statutes &mdash; NDPS included.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports an order of the High Court of Delhi for general information only. It is not legal advice and does not comment on any pending proceeding.</div>
